{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat preread-brief.md preread-packet-2.json'","aggregated_output":"# Gate 1 pre-read: which matters in a thesis meet the opening tests (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Read only this brief and `preread-packet.json`. Do not open other files, run commands or model calls.\n\nEach item is a thesis a research step opened from the article shown. Its outcome may name one matter or several. For each item:\n1. List every **matter** the thesis (question, outcome, period) tracks or names as something that could be tracked: the main one,\n   and any second quantity, period or event. Do not list a reference figure it is compared with, the main event's own other outcome\n   (a slip to a later date, an abandonment, a bar) or context (a fine, a consequence): those belong to the main matter.\n2. For each matter, decide whether it **meets the opening tests on its own**: a senior hedge-fund analyst would hold a separate card\n   for it, because its answer could change the company's revenue, profit, cash, credit risk or competitive position; public material\n   will settle it on a date; and it is not the same question as another matter listed.\n\nReturn one JSON object per item, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else:\n`{\"n\": 1, \"matters\": [{\"matter\": \"…\", \"main\": true|false, \"meets_tests\": true|false, \"why\": \"one sentence\"}]}`.\n[\n {\n  \"n\": 1,\n  \"question\": \"Will NAND demand generated by Nvidia's CMX (Context Memory Storage) architecture, the storage tier built around its BlueField-4 DPU for long-context and agentic inference, exceed 100 million TB in calendar 2027, up from a projected 35 million TB in 2026?\",\n  \"outcome\": \"The volume of NAND demand attributable to Nvidia's CMX architecture in calendar 2027, against the item's projection of more than 100 million TB (from 35 million TB in 2026), and whether memory makers' capacity additions keep pace with it.\",\n  \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 with calendar 2026 as base year; settled when full-year 2027 NAND demand/bit-shipment data for CMX are published (early 2028, e.g. TrendForce releases and memory makers' 2027 reports).\",\n  \"article\": {\n   \"title\": \"[News] Kioxia Reportedly to Sample CXL Modules Soon, Reaffirms Strong NVIDIA CMX Ties\",\n   \"date\": \"2026-09-07\",\n   \"text\": \"[News] Kioxia Reportedly to Sample CXL Modules Soon, Reaffirms Strong NVIDIA CMX Ties\\nAs AI workloads demand ever more memory, DRAM’s physical capacity ceiling is becoming the bottleneck. At a September 3rd briefing, Japan’s NAND giant detailed how its CXL memory modules expand usable memory capacity while cutting latency by over 90% versus conventional approaches, per EE Times Japan .\\nAccording to EE Times Japan, Kioxia plans to begin CXL memory module sampling soon.\\nCiting Katsuki Matsudera, head of Memory Application Technology at Kioxia’s Memory Division, the report further notes that simulations showed that replacing one-third of DRAM with CXL memory modules would keep performance degradation to less than 5%. Alternatively, replacing part of DRAM with CXL memory modules at the same cost could double capacity while boosting performance by 1.3x, the report adds.\\nAs the report notes, AI is driving strong DRAM demand, but DRAM’s limited capacity makes it difficult to scale memory within increasingly data-hungry systems. NAND flash offers far greater capacity, yet comes with its own trade-offs: higher latency and lower write endurance make it unsuitable as a direct replacement for DRAM in main memory.\\nAccording to Matsudera, real-world access patterns follow an 80/20 split: roughly 80% of memory accesses hit just 20% of the data, while the rest is accessed far less often. Kioxia’s approach exploits that gap — storing the 80% on flash memory that, like DRAM, can be accessed directly as memory, easing capacity constraints without sacrificing performance.\\nBut flash’s inherent latency remained a hurdle. To close that gap, Kioxia developed a dedicated controller with latency-reduction features. Paired with XL-FLASH, the resulting CXL memory module cuts latency by more than 90% compared with conventional approaches, per EE Times Japan.\\nBeyond CXL, Kioxia also used the briefing to update its flagship BiCS Flash roadmap. The company began mass production of its 10th-generation BiCS FLASH 3D flash memory at its Kitakami Plant in Iwate starting in July, while also shipping samples of its 1Tb TLC products. Compared with the 8th generation, the new generation delivers a 59% increase in bit density, a 40% improvement in read power efficiency, and a 30% improvement in write power efficiency, per EE Times Japan.\\nNotably, BigGo Finance also highlights Kioxia’s reported collaboration with NVIDIA around CMX (Context Memory Storage) — a new storage tier NVIDIA announced in March, built around its BlueField-4 data processing unit to expand GPU memory for long-context conversational and agentic AI inference.\\nThat collaboration is already bearing fruit: Kioxia unveiled the CM10 Series in July, its latest enterprise SSDs powered by 10th-generation BiCS FLASH TLC and built to support the NVIDIA CMX architecture, delivering major gains in performance and power efficiency over the previous generation.\\nAs Kioxia’s first PCIe 6.0 enterprise SSD, the CM10 Series also supports direct-cold-plate liquid cooling, offering greater flexibility and efficiency for cooling next-generation AI infrastructure, the report adds.\\nAccording to the report, Makoto Hamada, General Manager of the SSD Business Division’s Technology Planning Department, noted that the competition is inevitable given the market’s huge potential, but said Kioxia remains closely engaged with NVIDIA and regularly communicates with key executives. “I don’t feel we are falling behind,” he said, adding that Kioxia is also watching emerging storage needs in AI PCs and physical AI, the report suggests.\\nAs SeDaily points out, Samsung could be another key player in the market, as the South Korean memory giant is reportedly accelerating its V9 NAND capacity expansion, driven in part by NVIDIA’s next-generation storage architecture, CMX, slated for launch in the second half of the year.\\nAccording to SeDaily, NVIDIA’s CMX packs 576 SSDs for 9,600TB of total capacity, while NAND demand for the architecture is projected to surge from 35 million TB this year to more than 100 million TB next year. With Samsung’s NAND output expected to reach around 250 million TB this year, the company is positioning its massive production capacity to capture a larger share of the emerging CMX market, the report adds.\\n[News] NVIDIA Vera Rubin Spillover from HBM to NAND: CMX Fuels TLC Spot Price Rebound from June Dip\\n[News] Kioxia Steps Up AI NAND Competition With South Korean Rivals, Eyes PCIe 6.0 and UFS 5.0 Mass Production\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 2,\n  \"question\": \"Will France's competition authority reach a conclusion/decision — a fine, behavioral remedies or closure — in its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA's alleged anti-competitive practices in the cloud-AI and GPU markets by NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027)?\",\n  \"outcome\": \"The French competition authority's decision concluding its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA, including any fine or behavioral remedies imposed.\",\n  \"period\": \"NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision; the item gives no date for the conclusion, only that it is 'nearing a conclusion'.\",\n  \"article\": {\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Bears Add to Wagers as Competition Threatens Its Dominance: Short Seller Tracker\",\n   \"date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n   \"text\": \"Meta Says AI Compute Isn’t Enough: Is the Infrastructure Selloff Over?\\n5.3M Views · 140 Posts\\n$NVIDIA (NVDA.US)$ faced mounting pressure after bears added to their wagers against the chip giant amid increasing competition that threatened to challenge its dominance in the market for semiconductors that power artificial intelligence workloads.\\nTrading in borrowed Nvidia shares that were sold short rose to 13.61 million shares Wednesday, from 11.69 million shares a day earlier, exchange data tracked by moomoo showed. The latest figure represented 9.2% of the shares that changed hands yesterday, when the stock advanced 3.7%, the biggest gain in five weeks. Shorting involves shares sold without the seller owning them, which is a common practice used to profit from a potential decline in a stock's price.\\n$Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo. The social media giant that’s looking to compete with $Amazon (AMZN.US)$ , $Microsoft (MSFT.US)$ and $Alphabet-A (GOOGL.US)$ in providing AI cloud services, is working with $Broadcom (AVGO.US)$ to design and $Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM.US)$ to manufacture the AI chips, according to the report.\\nMeta is Nvidia’s fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, $Super Micro Computer (SMCI.US)$ and Alphabet, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\nStill, sales of Nvidia’s Taiwanese assembly partners climbed in June, signaling continued strong demand. Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\n$HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\\nNVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\nThe stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\\nRegulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\\nShare your thoughts on Nvidia in the comments section. Are retail investors losing their faith in Nvidia? Let your voice be heard by voting below.\\nComments (4)\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 3,\n  \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n  \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\",\n  \"period\": \"Calendar 2026-2027 memory contract cycles; set by the item's forward cost period (second half of 2026 and beyond).\",\n  \"article\": {\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n   \"date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia's Next AI Rack Costs Nearly Double the Last One, and Memory Is Why. What Infrastructure Buyers Should Budget For.\\nNvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%. This shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams put it to work with Executive Thought Leadership .\\nKey facts, context, and what it means.\\nMemory costs jumped 435% to roughly $2 million per rack due to increased LPDDR5X capacity and 3D NAND storage, making it a strategic budget variable across all technology purchases\\nGPU share of bill of materials fell from 63% to 51% while memory rose to 25-30%, requiring infrastructure planners to model AI spend on total system cost not accelerator count\\nMemory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\\nTurn your Software & Technology expertise into content.\\nWhat infrastructure planners should account for\\nThe single most expensive part of Nvidia's next-generation AI rack is no longer just the GPU. According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation. For any organization budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond, that shift changes how the numbers should be built.\\nMorgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\nThe memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\\nAs a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\\nOnly a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\\nFor enterprise technology buyers, that means memory has become a strategic budget variable rather than a rounding error, whether the purchase is a data center commitment or a fleet refresh.\\nThree considerations follow directly from the Morgan Stanley breakdown.\\nModel your AI spend on total system cost, not GPU count. The generation-over-generation story is that peripheral components, memory, printed circuit boards, networking, cooling, and power, are absorbing a larger share of every dollar. Budgets built around GPU headline prices will understate the real bill.\\nTreat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\\nRevisit the build-versus-rent math. As rack prices climb toward $8 million and memory pricing stays volatile, the calculus between owning infrastructure and consuming cloud capacity shifts. Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\\nNvidia's Vera Rubin platform will deliver a step change in performance, and demand is not in question. What has changed is where the money goes. The AI infrastructure bill is increasingly a memory bill, and the organizations planning capacity for the next two years will be the ones that budget for the full system rather than the chip on the front of the box.\\nNvidia's memory costs soar 485%, latest AI systems now cost $7.8 million to build ↗ · Tom's Hardware\\nNvidia Vera Rubin rack hit with 435% memory price surge ↗ · Wccftech\\nNvidia's $7.8 million Vera Rubin rack rewrites AI profit map as GPU share shrinks ↗ · BigGo Finance\\nNVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack memory costs explode as HBM4 and LPDDR5X prices cross $2 million ↗ · OnMSFT\\nEvery story in MarketScale Software & Technology starts with a company putting its solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers on the record. Buyers are already reading this topic. The only question is whose experts they find.\\nBuyers ask AI engines who to consider, and published expert answers are what those engines cite.\\nMarketScale gives Software & Technology B2B marketing teams a full content studio: record, produce, and distribute your own channel. No agency, no crew, no guessing.\\nBefore they reach out, Software & Technology buyers ask AI engines which vendors to trust. Explore how your experts, customers, and partners can become useful content for buyers and AI search.\\nThis article was produced through MarketScale. The same platform turns your solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers into the articles, video, and social content Software & Technology buyers are searching for. Start a free trial and see it with your own people. For qualifying companies, no credit card, no demo required.\\nUber deployed ChatGPT Work to more than 30,000 employees seven weeks after cutting about 3,300 jobs (10% of corporate staff). The company framed layoffs as organizational restructuring, while the rollout expands a pilot that embedded AI engineers with teams to automate pieces of their work.\\n01 Uber expanded ChatGPT Work from a 1,000-person pilot to over 30,000 employees across operations, finance, marketing, and product teams.\\n02 During the pilot, 80% of early adopters were non-engineers, and AI pods of 30 engineers paired with teams to automate work such as analyzing operational data, localizing training materials, and building dashboards.\\n03 Uber’s rollout shows AI deployments can go beyond licenses: curated integrations, embedded technical support, an internal skills library, and plans to measure adoption and outcomes.\\nTwo critical unauthenticated remote code execution flaws in PHP (CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619) with CVSS 9.2 scores affected 34.4 million vulnerable services as of October 23, with no patches available for PHP 8.1 and older. Exploitation attempts reached millions within hours of disclosure, though confirmed compromises remain unproven, while attackers tested a Mirai-derived botnet variant against production servers.\\nUNICOM Engineering chief technologist Austin Hypes compares rear door, single-phase, two-phase and immersion cooling for AI racks on Flawless Execution. He says two-phase cold plates use dielectric refrigerant and can avoid chillers and evaporative towers in hot, dry sites, and immersion can keep servers up ten to 15 minutes and sometimes an hour or more after a cooling loss. Schneider Electric engineers cite estimates that single-phase holds about 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market.\\n01 A Motivair by Schneider Electric engineer puts single-phase cold plates at an estimated 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market and expects them to dominate for five to 10 years.\\nStories like this one run on content MarketScale captures from real practitioners. See how your team's expertise becomes coverage in Software & Technology and beyond.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 4,\n  \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n  \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\",\n  \"period\": \"First shipments by end of calendar 2028 — set by the item: SemiAnalysis says the Kyber NVL144 architecture 'has been delayed to 2028' from a planned 2027 debut alongside Rubin Ultra.\",\n  \"article\": {\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n   \"date\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"text\": \"Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026.\\nNvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\\nNvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\nShares of the artificial intelligence chip giant climbed about 1% on Monday.\\nKyber is a server cabinet that packs 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips into a single unit so they can work together as one giant computer, providing the horsepower AI companies need to train and run their most advanced models.\\nThe design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\\nThe setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\\n\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\\nNVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\\nThe reported delay adds to mounting strains across Nvidia's product lines, underscoring concerns that Nvidia's breakneck annual release cadence is colliding with manufacturing limits.\\nA backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\\nThat leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\\nThe delays \\\"should not be over analyzed as affecting the long-term criticality of Nvidia to AI data infrastructure buildouts,\\\" said Paul Triolo, a partner at consultancy DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group, noting the company \\\"has faced these kinds of challenges before, and has worked with vendors to overcome technical issues.\\\"\\nWith power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \\\"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\\\"\\nNvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud. SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\\nWhile Huawei and other domestic manufacturers \\\"will potentially gain some time,\\\" China's hardware ecosystem is increasingly diverging from — rather than chasing — the Nvidia-led model, Triolo said. \\\"The issue is no longer really catching up, but how good will China's alternative AI stack be by 2030,\\\" he said.\"\n  }\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"n\":1,\"matters\":[{\"matter\":\"Whether CMX generates more than 100 million TB of NAND demand in calendar 2027\",\"main\":true,\"meets_tests\":true,\"why\":\"The demand could affect memory suppliers' revenue and competitive position, and the item identifies full-year 2027 data as the way to settle it.\"},{\"matter\":\"Whether memory makers' capacity additions keep pace with CMX demand in 2027\",\"main\":false,\"meets_tests\":true,\"why\":\"Supply keeping pace is a separate question that could affect pricing and suppliers' profits, with 2027 capacity and demand data available afterward.\"}]}\n{\"n\":2,\"matters\":[{\"matter\":\"Whether the French competition authority concludes its NVIDIA investigation by late January 2027\",\"main\":true,\"meets_tests\":true,\"why\":\"The decision could affect NVIDIA's competitive position, and the authority's public actions can settle whether it concluded by the stated date.\"}]}\n{\"n\":3,\"matters\":[{\"matter\":\"LPDDR5X and other DRAM contract prices during 2026–2027\",\"main\":true,\"meets_tests\":true,\"why\":\"Memory prices can affect rack costs and demand, and public market reports can track them through the stated period.\"},{\"matter\":\"HBM contract prices during 2026–2027\",\"main\":false,\"meets_tests\":true,\"why\":\"HBM has its own supply and pricing and can affect AI system economics, with market reports available over the stated period.\"},{\"matter\":\"3D NAND contract prices during 2026–2027\",\"main\":false,\"meets_tests\":true,\"why\":\"NAND prices are a separate input that can affect rack costs and demand, and market reports can track them through the stated period.\"},{\"matter\":\"Memory content cost per Vera Rubin rack during 2026–2027\",\"main\":false,\"meets_tests\":true,\"why\":\"The combined per-rack cost can affect system pricing and demand and can be tracked through published cost estimates over the stated period.\"}]}\n{\"n\":4,\"matters\":[{\"matter\":\"Whether Kyber NVL144 first ships by the end of 2028\",\"main\":true,\"meets_tests\":true,\"why\":\"Shipment timing could affect NVIDIA's revenue and competitive position, and public shipment announcements can settle it by the stated date.\"},{\"matter\":\"Whether the larger NVL576 system ships in volume\",\"main\":false,\"meets_tests\":false,\"why\":\"Its volume shipments could affect NVIDIA's competitive position, but the item gives no date by which that separate question must be settled.\"}]}"}}
